WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:01.710
Instructor: All right, so before we go on

00:01.710 --> 00:03.900
to installation, we're about to do that,

00:03.900 --> 00:06.900
we need to start by talking about the two options

00:06.900 --> 00:08.880
for how we interact with Git.

00:08.880 --> 00:12.240
We can use the terminal, a text-based interface,

00:12.240 --> 00:15.630
and we can use graphical user interfaces.

00:15.630 --> 00:17.730
So, since it was originally created

00:17.730 --> 00:20.850
and still to this day, Git is largely,

00:20.850 --> 00:25.410
or primarily a terminal based tool, a text-based interface.

00:25.410 --> 00:28.830
It's used mostly by developers and has been for a long time.

00:28.830 --> 00:32.550
And most developers are comfortable using the command line.

00:32.550 --> 00:34.380
So, it's not the most user friendly experience.

00:34.380 --> 00:36.030
It's certainly not the most beautiful,

00:36.030 --> 00:37.650
but it's at the core of Git.

00:37.650 --> 00:40.500
It's what Git was really built to do originally.

00:40.500 --> 00:43.860
With that said, as Git's popularity has increased,

00:43.860 --> 00:45.750
so have a number of GUIs,

00:45.750 --> 00:48.510
graphical user interfaces, applications,

00:48.510 --> 00:51.600
pieces of software you download that make it easier,

00:51.600 --> 00:53.610
or at least more visual

00:53.610 --> 00:56.340
and more graphical to interact with Git.

00:56.340 --> 00:59.100
There are quite a few of these different applications

00:59.100 --> 01:02.490
out there, these different GUIs for all different platforms.

01:02.490 --> 01:05.070
Some of the more common ones are GitHub Desktop

01:05.070 --> 01:09.030
SourceTree, Tower, GitKraken, Ungit.

01:09.030 --> 01:12.960
There's actually a whole list here on the Git website.

01:12.960 --> 01:15.330
Can see quite a few of them.

01:15.330 --> 01:17.550
Some of them are free, some of them are not.

01:17.550 --> 01:18.840
Some of them you have to buy.

01:18.840 --> 01:22.350
Some of them are subscriptions or they have different tiers,

01:22.350 --> 01:25.260
but they all have the same goal, to help visualize,

01:25.260 --> 01:27.870
and not just like visually show you stuff,

01:27.870 --> 01:30.810
but help you interact with Gits using your mouse

01:30.810 --> 01:33.960
and buttons instead of using the terminal.

01:33.960 --> 01:35.790
So, just as a quick example.

01:35.790 --> 01:39.185
Here, I have a Git project open using my terminal.

01:39.185 --> 01:42.810
If I want to see a history of all the different people

01:42.810 --> 01:45.840
who have collaborated or all the different checkpoints,

01:45.840 --> 01:46.950
here they all are.

01:46.950 --> 01:48.900
This is what it looks like in my terminal.

01:48.900 --> 01:52.020
This is a graphical user interface.

01:52.020 --> 01:52.980
It's a lot prettier.

01:52.980 --> 01:55.050
It's the exact same project.

01:55.050 --> 01:56.763
2048 is the name of it.

01:57.900 --> 02:00.960
This is much prettier and in some ways

02:00.960 --> 02:02.910
a lot easier to understand.

02:02.910 --> 02:06.810
However, it may not be faster in all situations.

02:06.810 --> 02:08.760
For somebody who's well versed with a terminal,

02:08.760 --> 02:11.280
with a command line who's already using it day-to-day,

02:11.280 --> 02:12.960
this is still fastest.

02:12.960 --> 02:14.490
It's what's most comfortable.

02:14.490 --> 02:16.710
It takes time to get to that point.

02:16.710 --> 02:18.240
And I'm not telling you, you know,

02:18.240 --> 02:20.040
one way or the other is better,

02:20.040 --> 02:23.490
but using a terminal is going to be expected of you

02:23.490 --> 02:26.610
if you are a developer or if you plan on being a developer

02:26.610 --> 02:29.310
and you can do things in one line of code here,

02:29.310 --> 02:32.413
one command, that might take three or four button clicks,

02:32.413 --> 02:35.010
but at the same time, this opens the doors

02:35.010 --> 02:38.130
to all sorts of people to use Git and interact with Git

02:38.130 --> 02:40.500
who aren't super command line savvy, right?

02:40.500 --> 02:43.590
This is a pretty terrible interface, objectively.

02:43.590 --> 02:46.209
So, I'll just quickly run through sort of the pros and cons.

02:46.209 --> 02:48.570
Using these graphical user interfaces,

02:48.570 --> 02:50.910
there's a much lower barrier of entry, that's always great.

02:50.910 --> 02:54.570
Friendlier to use, can be a much more positive experience.

02:54.570 --> 02:56.820
And for some users who are experienced

02:56.820 --> 02:59.280
with the command line, they still may prefer

02:59.280 --> 03:01.650
the visual experience of a GUI.

03:01.650 --> 03:04.920
Now, some of the cons are that there's a lot of magic

03:04.920 --> 03:07.230
involved with the graphical user interfaces.

03:07.230 --> 03:08.640
Some of them really obfuscate

03:08.640 --> 03:12.210
or they abstract away what's happening behind the scenes.

03:12.210 --> 03:14.820
So, you may not really understand what your button clicks

03:14.820 --> 03:18.300
are doing, and that can work for you 99% of the time,

03:18.300 --> 03:20.655
but then when things go wrong, it can be a problem.

03:20.655 --> 03:24.330
Also, these GUIs lead, can lead, to users

03:24.330 --> 03:26.940
being dependent on one piece of software

03:26.940 --> 03:29.760
because they do change one GUI to the next.

03:29.760 --> 03:32.910
Even if they are similar, they have different buttons,

03:32.910 --> 03:35.940
different approaches, different labels for things,

03:35.940 --> 03:37.650
different windows and all of that.

03:37.650 --> 03:42.270
So, eh, you know, I'm very pro GUI, to be honest with you.

03:42.270 --> 03:45.810
However, the command line has a lot going for it as well

03:45.810 --> 03:47.670
if you can get past the fact

03:47.670 --> 03:49.020
that it's not beginner friendly,

03:49.020 --> 03:52.680
it's ugly and it's often not the best experience

03:52.680 --> 03:54.540
if you're designing it from scratch.

03:54.540 --> 03:57.603
If you can get past those cons, it can be a lot faster,

03:58.560 --> 03:59.970
once you get comfortable.

03:59.970 --> 04:02.610
There are some features that you can really only do

04:02.610 --> 04:05.400
or interact with on the command line, Git features,

04:05.400 --> 04:07.030
but also a whole bunch of other features.

04:07.030 --> 04:09.210
The commands are always going to be the same

04:09.210 --> 04:10.980
from one command line to the next.

04:10.980 --> 04:13.980
Assuming you're using a Unix space shell,

04:13.980 --> 04:15.630
it doesn't matter whose machine you're on

04:15.630 --> 04:18.780
or which machine you're on, the commands are consistent

04:18.780 --> 04:22.050
and Git is created as a command line tool.

04:22.050 --> 04:25.680
So, all the documentation, tutorials, resources,

04:25.680 --> 04:28.440
they're all oriented around the command line.

04:28.440 --> 04:30.480
So, there's a lot going for both sides.

04:30.480 --> 04:33.510
Unfortunately, there's kind of a lot of gatekeeping

04:33.510 --> 04:36.259
around people using GUIs, that it's somehow lesser

04:36.259 --> 04:38.370
or you're not really using Git.

04:38.370 --> 04:41.160
It's kind of garbage, it's BS in my opinion.

04:41.160 --> 04:45.300
We will be using both in this course, not side by side,

04:45.300 --> 04:48.510
but rather, I will be teaching using the command line.

04:48.510 --> 04:51.570
That's really the assumed way of interacting with Git

04:51.570 --> 04:52.950
still to this day.

04:52.950 --> 04:54.660
If you have plans on being a developer,

04:54.660 --> 04:56.160
if you are a developer already,

04:56.160 --> 04:58.230
command line is just part of that package.

04:58.230 --> 05:00.030
You've gotta know how to interact with it.

05:00.030 --> 05:02.640
So, I will be showing you how to use Git

05:02.640 --> 05:04.380
from the command line,

05:04.380 --> 05:08.160
but I'll also be illustrating concepts using a GUI.

05:08.160 --> 05:10.200
I will be translating what we do.

05:10.200 --> 05:12.150
So, I'll show you, you know, here's this one thing

05:12.150 --> 05:14.190
we can do where we make a new branch,

05:14.190 --> 05:16.650
or here's how we merge, blah, blah, blah.

05:16.650 --> 05:20.520
And then, here's how you can do the same thing in a GUI.

05:20.520 --> 05:23.940
So, we'll be using a GUI in particular called GitKraken.

05:23.940 --> 05:26.490
This is this lovely logo here.

05:26.490 --> 05:29.040
We will be installing it and setting it up in a video

05:29.040 --> 05:30.000
in this section,

05:30.000 --> 05:32.850
but first we're going to install Git on our command line.

05:32.850 --> 05:34.920
So, if you're on a Mac, there'll be a video

05:34.920 --> 05:38.220
for installing Git on a Mac, and if you're on a PC,

05:38.220 --> 05:41.520
there'll be a video for installing Git on a Windows machine.

05:41.520 --> 05:44.190
All righty, so hopefully that explains a little bit

05:44.190 --> 05:46.650
about the differences or the two ways

05:46.650 --> 05:47.850
of interacting with Git.

05:47.850 --> 05:52.650
There's the hideous, but very, very common command line,

05:52.650 --> 05:54.930
ubiquitous amongst Git users.

05:54.930 --> 05:57.540
And then, there's this fractured world of user interfaces,

05:57.540 --> 06:00.480
graphical user interfaces that vary quite a bit.

06:00.480 --> 06:02.310
Some of them aren't full featured,

06:02.310 --> 06:05.520
others are quite powerful, but they all have the goal

06:05.520 --> 06:09.120
of making it easier or more visual to work with Git.

06:09.120 --> 06:10.290
So, we'll be using both,

06:10.290 --> 06:12.450
but primarily the command line first.

06:12.450 --> 06:14.550
And I will actually have a crash course

06:14.550 --> 06:17.300
on how to use the command line in this section as well.
